by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\nReproducible in Godot 4.8.dev (master), Godot 4.7.2.stable, Godot 4.7.stable, Godot 4.6.3.stable, Godot 4.5.1.stable, Godot 4.4.1.stable\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.7.2.stable - Windows 11 (build 26200) - Multi-window, 2 monitors - Direct3D 12 (Mobile) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Ti (NVIDIA; 32.0.16.1062) - Intel(R) Core(TM) Ultra 7 265K (20 threads) - 63.37 GiB memory - WASAPI (48000 Hz, Stereo/mono)\n\n### Issue description\n\nIn the 2D scene view, you can right click with a selected node and click 'Move Node(s) Here', which moves the nodes to a specific position in mouse coordinates. That is the intended behavior.\n\nHowever, when you have a scaled object as a child of another object that's in a position other than 0,0, it doesn't properly move to that position. \n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/5a69db41-7ad9-44ac-9e48-3a06ffb2689c\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n- Create a 2D Scene.\n- Create a Node2D that is a child of the Node2D scene root. Move it anywhere that isn't 0,0.\n- Create a Sprite2D (or any Node2D) that is a child of the previously created Node2D.\n- Scale the Sprite2D by any scale, typically a larger scale.\n- With the Sprite2D selected, right click anywhere in the viewport you want the Sprite2D to be in, and click \"Move Node(s) Here\". At this point, is where the issue will occur.\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/27a37cac-11cb-423c-a99f-e1c90e9563fa\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nN/A","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17489292?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo","html_url":"https://github.com/Repiteo","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com